---
import CuisTargetPanels from '../../../../components/CuisTargetPanels.astro';
import Layout from '../../../../components/Layout.astro';
import SourceList from '../../../../components/SourceList.astro';
import { cuisGroups, cuisGroupPath, cuisTransport } from '../../../../data/cuis-calls';
import { manualBreadcrumbs } from '../../../../data/manual-structure';
import abi from '../../../../data/abi.json';
---
<Layout title="CUIS" description="Core User Interface Services and the pTOS syscall ABI used by userspace libaes."
  running="AES" breadcrumbs={manualBreadcrumbs('aes')}>
  <header class="directory-head"><h1>CUIS</h1><p>Core User Interface Services</p></header>
  <aside class="prm-notice" aria-label="CUIS development status">
    <strong>Development warning.</strong> CUIS is under heavy development. Its API and syscall ABI may change.
  </aside>
  <p>CUIS is the kernel backend for the experimental userspace <code>libaes.so</code>.
    The library invokes CUIS through an architecture-specific syscall ABI. CUIS coordinates shared state such as
    endpoint ownership, window stacking, clipping, focus, input and waits.</p>
  <p>Applications call the GEM API through GEMlib and libaes, starting with
    <a href="/reference/appl_init/"><code>appl_init</code></a>. libaes translates those calls into CUIS operations
    when it needs a kernel service. It handles object trees, resources, dialogs and GEM policy in the application’s
    address space. <a href="/subsystems/vdi/">VDI</a> provides drawing operations.</p>
  <p>CUIS currently runs only on x32 builds with <code>CONF_WITH_CUIS</code>. The proposed 68k and ARM conventions
    are described below.</p>
  <section aria-labelledby="cuis-groups">
    <h2 id="cuis-groups" class="prm-section-heading ruled">Call groups</h2>
    <ul class="manual-index">
      {cuisGroups.map(group => <li><a class="manual-index-symbol" href={cuisGroupPath(group.slug)}>{group.title}</a>
        <span>{group.description}</span></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>
  <CuisTargetPanels opcode={abi.cuis.sessionQuery} registers={[
    ['RDI, RSI, RDX, R10, R8, R9', 'Argument slots in order. Each call documents the slots it uses.'],
  ]}>
    <p>The selector diagram uses <a href="/reference/cuis-session-query/"><code>CUIS_SESSION_QUERY</code></a>
      as an example. The class occupies the upper 32 bits of RAX, and the operation occupies the lower 32 bits.
      Sign-extend signed scalar inputs such as coordinates when the call requires it. Use zero-extended x32 user
      addresses for pointers to buffers. Fixed-width outputs are little-endian.</p>
    <p>Endpoint handles, GUI client IDs, PROCESS numbers and MiNT PIDs name different objects.
      See <a href="/reference/cuis-peer-query/"><code>CUIS_PEER_QUERY</code></a> for client and PROCESS identity
      and <a href="/reference/s-procexec/"><code>S_PROCEXEC</code></a> for process observation.</p>
  </CuisTargetPanels>
  <SourceList sources={[
    ['ptos', 'doc/libaes-compatibility.md', 'GEM API coverage and userspace binding ownership'],
    ['ptos', 'include/cuis.h', 'CUIS service class and operation selectors'],
    ['ptos', cuisTransport, 'libaes x32 syscall transport'],
    ['ptos', 'bios/arch/x86_64/trap.c', 'Native dispatch and privilege checks'],
    ['ptos', 'aes/Kconfig', 'CUIS configuration and architecture constraints'],
    ['ptos', 'doc/cuis.txt', 'CUIS and libaes implementation contracts'],
    ['ptos', 'tests/cuis/program.c', 'Native syscall and invalid-handle probes'],
  ]} />
</Layout>
